  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Yew Crust (Meruliopsis taxicola) is a small, inconspicuous wood-decay fungus belonging to the Irpicaceae family, characterized by its thin, delicate fruiting bodies. This species is specialized in decomposing the wood of yew trees (Taxus species), making it a notable indicator of yew tree health and decay.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Host Specificity",
      "description": "Found almost exclusively on yew (Taxus) wood, which is an extremely reliable identifying characteristic",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Texture and Color",
      "description": "Features a pale cream to whitish, delicate merulioid (wrinkled) hymenium surface that may appear pore-like or gill-like in structure",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Thin Fruiting Body",
      "description": "Forms a very thin, tightly adherent crust on the wood surface, often difficult to separate from the substrate",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Meruliopsis taxicola is found on dead or dying yew wood in moist, shaded woodlands throughout New England, typically on fallen branches or dead portions of living yew trees.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "This fungus is so specialized on yew wood that it serves as a bioindicator of yew tree presence and decay, making it useful for forest ecologists studying these relatively uncommon native trees in New England forests.",
